{
 "id": "a01",
 "label": "A1",
 "title": "Foods",
 "summary": "Market words for food: vegetables (talóng, kamátis, sibúyas…), fish and shellfish (bangús, sugpô, alamáng), noodles (bíhon, cantón), meats (karné ng baka, karné ng baboy), poultry (manók, itlóg) and spices (patís, toyò, sukà)",
 "minutes": 35,
 "goals": [
  "Ask for vegetables in a wet market by their Tagalog names",
  "Name common fish, shellfish and noodles",
  "Name the main meats and poultry, including eggs",
  "Recognise the spices and condiments of a Filipino kitchen",
  "Spot the ng pattern in names such as karné ng baka and itlóg ng pugò"
 ],
 "sections": [
  {
   "title": "Shopping in a wet market",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Supermarkets in the Philippines usually label their products in English, but the **wet markets** are cheaper and have a wider variety, and there is also the experience of **haggling** over prices. The catch: many market vendors do not know the English names for what they sell."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "This appendix gives you the Tagalog names of everyday food items, sorted the way a market is: vegetables, fish and shellfish, noodles, meats, poultry, and spices and condiments."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Some English names work",
     "t": "A number of food items are simply known by their **English names**, for example carrot, cauliflower, greenshells and yellow fin tuna."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "culture",
     "title": "Accents in this list",
     "t": "The list prints accent marks to show stress. A few words appear twice with different marks (for example **bawang** and **báwang** for garlic). Each word below is shown the way the list prints it, with a note where the book prints another form."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Vegetables (1)",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The first half of the vegetable list, from bamboo shoots to long beans."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Already learned",
     "t": "**Cashew nut = kasúy** is also on this list; you met kasúy in Lesson 1."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "veg1",
     "title": "Vegetables: bamboo shoots to long beans"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Vegetables (2)",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The second half of the vegetable list, from Malabar spinach to yellow squash."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Words built from other words",
     "items": [
      "**pusó ng ságing** (banana heart) uses **ng**, the of-marker from Lesson 16.",
      "**kamóteng káhoy** (cassava) is **kamóte** (sweet potato) + the ligature **-ng** + **káhoy**.",
      "Onions: **sibúyas** (onion), **sibúyas Tagalog** (native onions), and **sibúyas na múra** or **sibúyas dáhon** (spring onions/shallots)."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "veg2",
     "title": "Vegetables: Malabar spinach to yellow squash"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Fish and shellfish",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Fish, shrimps, crabs and shellfish. Note that the two kinds of catfish have different names: **hitò** (freshwater) and **kandulì** (saltwater)."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "English",
      "Tagalog"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Small shrimps",
       "[[alamáng|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "Dried shrimp",
       "[[híbi|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "River shrimps",
       "[[uláng|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "Prawn",
       "[[sugpô|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "Crab",
       "[[alimasag|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "Mud crab",
       "[[alimángo|noun]]"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Shrimps, prawns and crabs"
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "fish1",
     "title": "Fish and shellfish: anchovy to milkfish"
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "fish2",
     "title": "Fish and shellfish: mud crab to tilapia"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Noodles",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "Noodle dishes are everywhere in the Philippines. These are the names of the noodles themselves."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "noodles",
     "title": "Noodles"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Meats",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Karné** means meat. Several meats are named with **karné ng** + the animal, using the of-marker **ng** (Lesson 16):"
    },
    {
     "type": "pattern",
     "title": "karné + ng + animal",
     "rows": [
      {
       "label": "beef",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "karné",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "ng",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "baka",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "label": "pork",
       "chips": [
        {
         "t": "karné",
         "r": "noun"
        },
        {
         "t": "ng",
         "r": "marker"
        },
        {
         "t": "baboy",
         "r": "noun"
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "note": "Ground meat: giníling na karné ng baka = ground beef; giníling na karné ng baboy = ground pork."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "meat",
     "title": "Meats"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Poultry and eggs",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Itlóg** is egg. The parts of an egg and the eggs of other birds are again made with **ng**:"
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "English",
      "Tagalog"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "Eggwhite",
       "[[putî|noun]] [[ng|marker]] [[itlóg|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "Egg yolk",
       "[[púla|noun]] [[ng|marker]] [[itlóg|noun]]"
      ],
      [
       "Quail egg",
       "[[itlóg|noun]] [[ng|marker]] [[pugò|noun]]"
      ]
     ],
     "title": "Egg words"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Colours inside food words",
     "t": "**Putî** is white and **pulá** is red (Lesson 44). The book prints the egg yolk as **púla ng itlóg**, with the accent on the first syllable."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "**Chicken = manók** heads the poultry list; you met manók in Lesson 15."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "poultry",
     "title": "Poultry"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "title": "Spices and condiments",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "t": "The flavours of the Filipino kitchen: fish sauce, soy sauce, vinegar, garlic and more."
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "tip",
     "title": "Colour words again",
     "t": "Green or red pepper is **síling bérde** or **síling pulá**: **síli** (chili) + **-ng** + the colour (bérde = green, pulá = red, Lesson 44). Turmeric, **lúyang diláw**, also contains a colour: **diláw** (yellow)."
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "spice1",
     "title": "Spices and condiments: anise wine to green or red pepper"
    },
    {
     "type": "vocab",
     "group": "spice2",
     "title": "Spices and condiments: lemongrass to vinegar"
    },
    {
     "type": "box",
     "kind": "remember",
     "title": "Remember",
     "items": [
      "Wet markets are cheaper and more varied, but vendors may only know the Tagalog names.",
      "Some foods keep their English names: carrot, cauliflower, greenshells, yellow fin tuna.",
      "karné = meat; karné ng baka = beef; karné ng baboy = pork.",
      "itlóg = egg; putî ng itlóg = eggwhite; púla ng itlóg = egg yolk.",
      "The big three condiments: patís (fish sauce), toyò (soy sauce), sukà (vinegar)."
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
